} {"question" "q-162201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/cfcbd4b374456b4a5913289592d875a42caf81fd02984180e68e34c5b82ef905.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3117cac283fb94cc19d5fe97a6acc1c3e0354b103cbb0237db3b15f187694112" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A line segment"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A point, different from the origin"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A rectangle"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A triangle"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3117cac283fb94cc19d5fe97a6acc1c3e0354b103cbb0237db3b15f187694112" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A line segment"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A point, different from the origin"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A rectangle"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A triangle"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A line segment"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/state.json" "file_sha256" "14476014320fedf4d1a6acd7b6131a6a9a2aeec40dc6c5bc972ee7ec8ac4d420" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,2), (0,1)$ and $(1,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$\n\n- $(-2,0)$\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(2,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12423.json" "file_sha256" "b47ae757f74c19467edaf19071d8ea9d8df05f6c4559a55bdf0ceb86ccfadf8c" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,0),$ $(0,0),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II,  and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12423.json" "file_sha256" "b47ae757f74c19467edaf19071d8ea9d8df05f6c4559a55bdf0ceb86ccfadf8c" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n  Find the image of the triangle shown below under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/853fd7d5c09944a5bacd0c2b3453504f3fa1eea9d8d3b2c3bdd4f0f44889dc16.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12424.json" "file_sha256" "155c5691fe02e425bc73640f7ce08367c0a0042428fd13851f92507c0a237579" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6), (1,-2),$ and $(-2,4).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/3fd1073b2eaa5aa9c21e537fcc4dc6cf538ab2992c0732f64da50dbcb63d8fd4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12424.json" "file_sha256" "155c5691fe02e425bc73640f7ce08367c0a0042428fd13851f92507c0a237579" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/89efcd0ecb51dac08a1102c5a347eea3459f7209de7cbac0a88b192259ca3b7e.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12438.json" "file_sha256" "bc6ec9a9a8eb97e889b4786ed77c98111a6db2ed94dd2ca7f39ed45f31ca9c97" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/example-12438.json" "file_sha256" "bc6ec9a9a8eb97e889b4786ed77c98111a6db2ed94dd2ca7f39ed45f31ca9c97" "basis" 1050 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/db7d2b33f2badad28b754d4482047a33bb37469ed8b7b17859b10552840e2f51.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,4)$, \n$(-6,12)$ and $(-1,2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/2e1dde461c6244a59672fc28c842b70a30297ac7a8c7e7cf2095a973cd1860cd.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cf444e4428213186917c4d85a01e25245d21b247f29d25a3832b05a20dac87fd" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0af059340bdef0548876baad9bcdf990f6bc92330617dde33c18c00578aecad1.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/461ff9fb5ef66239eb81314f761fac9ceb32b3bc88df6940b61445c752fe3d0c.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/bf7cc4e728cd1ade741c55b2fbb54720220e0e09a8521a150300532b4e3ee9ed.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/da61a7fd99ce683be4e66d3708ddf15c969b038f0250dc4c024ace870c3f7544.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/ed8a2354f8cc04f998875f599a52b50e6c64cafe8976f1bea176772543e295ac.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/8978d3bb02b4032b6e3a8b4a85878db647eb8323ac5b0423b5a9a6fab121c7e7.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-8)-4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cf444e4428213186917c4d85a01e25245d21b247f29d25a3832b05a20dac87fd" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A line segment"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A point, different from the origin"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A rectangle"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A triangle"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A line segment"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "7e9f67c493312b68153ed88582d196e9a43e9bc68dac7e9916cc5e5587c696eb" "basis" 1144 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-147668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,0)$, $(0,-3)$ and $(-2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(12,-24)$\n- $(8,16)$\n- $(2,-4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 12 & -8 \\\\ -4 & -24 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(2,-4)$, $(12,-24)$ and $(-8,16).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06739ce6498263f9c042f447fdb5e02ed1b51689c86067f26597e2e4e241dcd8"} {"question" "q-147668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,-1)$, $(1,0)$ and $(-2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$ \n\n- $(-1,2)$\n- $(-3,12)$\n- $(7,-12)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 12 & 2 & -14\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-6,12)$, $(-1,2)$ and $(7,-14).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I  only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06739ce6498263f9c042f447fdb5e02ed1b51689c86067f26597e2e4e241dcd8"} {"question" "q-147667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-96856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,-1),(1,1),$ and $(3,2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(-1,4)$\n- $(-2,4)$\n- $(1,-3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-96856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are  $(-2,4)$, and $(1,-2).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \" II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-96856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06739ce6498263f9c042f447fdb5e02ed1b51689c86067f26597e2e4e241dcd8"} {"question" "q-96856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-96856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-96856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-21068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,2)$, $(-1,2)$ and $(-1,0)$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(16,8)$\n- $(8,2)$\n- $(-4,4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-21068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$ \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(16,8)$, $(8,4)$ and $(-4,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-21068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06739ce6498263f9c042f447fdb5e02ed1b51689c86067f26597e2e4e241dcd8"} {"question" "q-21068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-21068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-21068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1),(0,2),$ and $(-2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(6,-1)$\n- $(7,-14)$\n- $(8,-16)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 12 & -14\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(8,-16),$ $(-6,12)$, and $(7,-14).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06739ce6498263f9c042f447fdb5e02ed1b51689c86067f26597e2e4e241dcd8"} {"question" "q-147671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-147671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-97022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/699e6ce441757dd21b01524b5405ab7f4b596bfe9ec6f8d0532a1e6f112eb8ad.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-97022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(1,2)$, $(2,4),$ $(3,6).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/791c82648482ef9932562150659ef74bb55df0216047ad56bb84f9bcf1b6ce4d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "7820ea54c2b2678a4b76a7d10a494f2c73230be4e5705e3c7f9dc05823c94b6d"} {"question" "q-97